Notebook[{

Cell[CellGroupData[{
Cell["Diagrams", "GuideTitle",
 CellID->942062912,ExpressionUUID->"a3a3c038-ca24-47b7-9945-83f1c1312fe3"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "In physics, computation and mathematics, inputs are related to outputs by \
compositional processes that compose in series and in parallel. These \
processes have a natural graphical syntax \[LongDash] ",
 StyleBox["string diagrams", "TI"],
 " \[LongDash] and the ",
 Cell[BoxData[
 "Wolfram"], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "f44614f2-bd1e-4b4e-86a7-e2cf877df1ad"],
 "DiagrammaticComputation`` paclet provides a uniform symbolic representation \
of them, along with tools for composing, transforming, rewriting, simplifying \
and visualising diagrams. A ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  ButtonBox["Diagram",
   BaseStyle->"Link",
   ButtonData->"paclet:Wolfram/DiagrammaticComputation/ref/Diagram"]], 
  "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->"609fde7d-f69d-41cd-ad60-00355b2f3846"],
 " is a morphism in a symmetric monoidal category with typed input and output \
",
 Cell[BoxData[
  ButtonBox["Port",
   BaseStyle->"Link",
   ButtonData->"paclet:Wolfram/DiagrammaticComputation/ref/Port"]], 
  "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->"ab7264c1-4333-4335-b17f-8884a9a088c2"],
 "s; everything else in this guide builds on those two primitives."
}], "GuideAbstract",
 CellID->2001916300,ExpressionUUID->"ef0ab37b-2e0d-4399-8f7e-82603ccbe925"]
}, Open  ]],

